.TH PILA 1 "" "Pila" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pila \- a stacking Wayland compositor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pila
.RB [ \-c | \-\-command
.IR CMD ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-backend
.BR winit | udev | headless ]
.RB [ \-\-headless\-outputs
.IR OUTPUTS ]
.br
.B pila \-\-check\-config
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B pila msg
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I request
.br
.B pila\-session
.br
.B pila\-report
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pila
is a stacking Wayland compositor: overlapping windows with title bars, snapping, workspaces
that span all monitors, animations and per-app rules, configured in one TOML file that applies
when saved.
X11 apps run through Xwayland, which Pila starts.
.PP
Started from a login screen or a TTY, Pila drives the monitors and input devices itself.
Started inside another desktop, it opens as a window there (nested), with Alt as its Mod key.
.PP
.B pila\-session
starts Pila as a login session: with its log in
.IR ~/.local/state/pila/pila.log ,
and a D-Bus session bus of its own (with dbus-run-session) when the system gives none.
Login screens run it; from a TTY, run it after logging in.
.PP
.B pila\-report
gathers what a bug report needs (version, system, GPU, monitors, whether the configuration is
valid, the end of the log) into
.I file
(default:
.IR pila\-report.txt ),
without the user name, host name and home directory.
It sends nothing anywhere.
.PP
The user manual,
.IR MANUAL.md ,
describes how to use and set up Pila; Pila's packages install it with the documentation
(usually in
.IR /usr/share/doc/pila ),
and it's on https://github.com/igalmarino/pila.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-command " " \fICMD\fR
Run the shell command
.I CMD
at startup, instead of the configuration's
.B autostart
(which defaults to the terminal).
.TP
.BR \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Read the configuration from
.I FILE
instead of the default (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.BR \-\-backend " " \fIBACKEND\fR
.B winit
opens Pila as a window in another Wayland or X11 session;
.B udev
drives the monitors and input devices directly, from a TTY;
.B headless
has virtual outputs and a control socket
.RI ( $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/ <WAYLAND_DISPLAY> .control ),
for automated tests.
The default is
.B winit
if
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
or
.B DISPLAY
is set, else
.BR udev .
.TP
.BR \-\-headless\-outputs " " \fIOUTPUTS\fR
The headless backend's outputs:
.IR NAME : W x H [@ HZ ][= MAKE / MODEL [/ SERIAL ]],
separated by commas (default: HEADLESS\-1:1280x800@60).
.TP
.B \-\-check\-config
Check the configuration file as Pila would read it, the keyboard layout included, print what's
wrong, and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of the options.
.SH PILA MSG
.B pila msg
talks to a running Pila:
.TP
.BR "pila msg " [ \-\-json ] " windows" | outputs | workspaces | keyboard | focused | version
List the windows, monitors, workspaces or keyboard layouts, or show the focused window or the
version.
.TP
.BR "pila msg " [ \-\-json ] " subscribe " [ windows | workspaces | outputs | keyboard ...]
Print events as they happen: the whole list of windows, workspaces, monitors or keyboard layouts
each time it changes, until Pila quits.
.TP
.BI "pila msg action " action
Run an action, as in a key binding (for example
.BR "workspace 2" ).
.TP
.BI "pila msg focus " id ", pila msg close " id
Focus a window, or ask it to close, by the
.I id
.B pila msg windows
gives.
.PP
Scripts should use
.BR \-\-json :
the plain text is for people and may change.
.B pila msg \-\-help
has the details.
Exit status: 0 on success, 1 if Pila refused the request, 2 for wrong arguments, 3 if it can't
reach Pila.
.SH DEFAULT BINDINGS
Mod is Super from a login screen or a TTY and Alt nested, unless the configuration's
.B mod
says otherwise.
.TP
.B Mod+Return
Open a terminal.
.TP
.B Mod+D
Open the launcher, if one is configured.
.TP
.B Mod
Tapped alone (pressed and released with no other key, click, scroll or gesture in between):
open the launcher too.
.TP
.BR Mod+Tab ", " Mod+J
Switch to the previous window; hold Mod and repeat for older ones.
.TP
.B Mod+Shift+Tab
The same, the other way.
.TP
.BR Alt+Tab ", " Alt+Shift+Tab
The same as Mod+Tab and Mod+Shift+Tab, whatever Mod is.
.TP
.BR Mod+M ", " Mod+Up
Maximize; again, restore.
.TP
.B Mod+N
Minimize.
.TP
.B Mod+F
Fullscreen.
.TP
.BR Mod+Left ", " Mod+Right
Fill the left or right half of the screen; again, restore.
.TP
.B Mod+Down
Restore a snapped or maximized window.
.TP
.BR Mod+Shift+Left ", " Mod+Shift+Right ", " Mod+Shift+Up ", " Mod+Shift+Down
Move the window.
.TP
.BR Mod+Ctrl+Right ", " Mod+Ctrl+Down ", " Mod+Ctrl+Left ", " Mod+Ctrl+Up
Make the window wider, taller, narrower, shorter.
.TP
.B Mod+C
Center the window.
.TP
.B Mod+T
Keep the window above the others; again, stop.
.TP
.B Mod+P
Keep the window on all workspaces; again, stop.
.TP
.B Mod+H
Minimize the workspace's windows; again, bring them back.
.TP
.BR Mod+O ", " Mod+Shift+O
Go to the next monitor; send the window there.
.TP
.BR Mod+1 " .. " Mod+9 ", " Mod+Shift+1 " .. " Mod+Shift+9
Go to a workspace; send the window there.
.TP
.BR Mod+Page_Down ", " Mod+Page_Up
Next, previous workspace.
.TP
.BR Mod+Shift+Page_Down ", " Mod+Shift+Page_Up
Send the window to the next, previous workspace.
.TP
.B Mod+Shift+S
Screenshot of a region to the clipboard (grim, slurp, wl-copy).
.TP
.B Mod+Ctrl+S
Screenshot of the monitor under the pointer to
.I ~/Pictures
(grim).
.TP
.B Mod+Shift+Q
Close the window.
.TP
.B Mod+Shift+E
Quit Pila.
.TP
.BR "Mod + left drag" ", " "Mod + right drag"
Move a window; resize it from the nearest corner.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Alt+F1 .. F12
Switch to another virtual terminal.
.PP
Touchpad swipes:
.B swipe\-3\-left
and
.B swipe\-3\-right
switch windows,
.B swipe\-3\-up
maximizes or restores,
.B swipe\-3\-down
restores,
.B swipe\-4\-left
and
.B swipe\-4\-right
go to the next and previous workspace.
.PP
The configuration's
.B [bindings]
and
.B [gestures]
change any of them but the mouse drags (which follow
.BR mod )
and the virtual terminal switch.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PILA_MOD
The Mod key:
.BR super ,
.B alt
or
.BR ctrl .
Overrides the configuration's
.BR mod .
.TP
.B PILA_TERMINAL
The terminal for Mod+Return and startup.
Overrides
.BR terminal .
.TP
.B PILA_LAUNCHER
The command for Mod+D and for tapping Mod alone.
Overrides
.BR launcher .
.TP
.BR XCURSOR_THEME ", " XCURSOR_SIZE
The cursor theme and size, from a TTY (default: default, 24).
.TP
.BR XKB_DEFAULT_LAYOUT ", " XKB_DEFAULT_VARIANT ", " XKB_DEFAULT_OPTIONS
The keyboard layout when the configuration's
.B [keyboard]
leaves it empty.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
What the log shows (default: info; debug for details).
.PP
Programs Pila starts get its
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
and Xwayland's
.BR DISPLAY ,
.BR XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP =pila,
.BR XDG_SESSION_TYPE =wayland
and
.B PILA_SOCKET
(for
.BR "pila msg" ),
and not other compositors' variables
.RB ( NIRI_SOCKET ", " SWAYSOCK ", ...),"
so a shell drives Pila;
commands run by key bindings also get
.BR PILA_OUTPUT ,
the name of the monitor under the pointer.
.PP
On a TTY, Pila exports
.BR WAYLAND_DISPLAY ", " DISPLAY ", " XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP ", " XDG_SESSION_TYPE
and
.B PILA_SOCKET
to the D\-Bus session bus (and to systemd \-\-user when that's the bus), for the
screen\-sharing portals and shells started as services.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR ~/.config/pila/config.toml " (or " $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pila/config.toml )
The configuration. Every option is described in
.IR config.example.toml ,
installed with the documentation by Pila's packages.
.TP
.IR ~/.local/state/pila/pila.log " (or " $XDG_STATE_HOME/pila/pila.log )
The log of a session started by
.BR pila\-session ;
.I pila.log.old
is the run before.
With systemd, also
.BR "journalctl \-t pila \-b" .
A crash is logged as "pila crashed", with where it happened.
.TP
.IR ~/.local/state/pila/sessions.json " (or " $XDG_STATE_HOME/pila/sessions.json )
Where windows were, for apps that ask to get them back (xdg\-session\-management).
A nested Pila, or a second one started next to a session, keeps that in memory only.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pila\-WAYLAND_DISPLAY.sock
The socket of
.BR "pila msg" ,
in
.B PILA_SOCKET
for the programs Pila starts.
.TP
.I /etc/xdg/xdg\-desktop\-portal/pila\-portals.conf
.TQ
.I /etc/xdg/xdg\-desktop\-portal\-wlr/pila
Which portals serve a Pila session, and how xdg\-desktop\-portal\-wlr picks what to share.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B pila
exits with 2 for a wrong argument and not 0 if it can't start.
.B pila \-\-check\-config
exits with 0 if the configuration is valid and 1 if not.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR Xwayland (1),
.BR xkeyboard\-config (7)
.PP
https://github.com/igalmarino/pila
